    "title": "'Cannot sit on bills indefinitely': SC's big guideline for Governors | India News",
    "text": "The Supreme Court on Thursday held that Governors cannot keep bills passed by state legislatures pending indefinitely, underscoring that such delays would undermine the constitutional scheme. However, the court refused to prescribe a mandatory timeline for Governors or the President to act on legislation, saying doing so would amount to overstepping judicial boundaries and “trampling upon the separation of powers”.The remarks came as the Constitution Bench prepares to deliver its verdict on a presidential reference seeking clarity on whether constitutional courts can direct the Governor or the President to decide on bills within a fixed time frame.A five-judge Bench comprising Chief Justice B R Gavai and Justices Surya Kant, Vikram Nath, P S Narasimha and A S Chandurkar had heard extensive arguments over 10 days before reserving the matter on September 11.The presidential reference was triggered in May, when President Droupadi Murmu invoked Article 143(1) to seek the Supreme Court’s opinion on whether courts can impose timelines for the exercise of gubernatorial and presidential discretion over state bills. Her request followed the Supreme Court’s April 8 ruling on the powers of the Tamil Nadu Governor in dealing with legislation passed by the state assembly.",
